<div class="seam-wrap" aria-hidden="true"><img class="seam" src="/cag-fs-seam-emblem.webp" alt="" width="182" height="60" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></div>

<section id="proven">
  <h2 class="cag-h2">What Does "Proven" Actually Mean on a Breeding Pair?</h2>
  <p>Proven means the pair has hatched a fertile clutch. Not paired, not seen mating, not two birds sitting shoulder to shoulder looking devoted — hatched, with chicks that came out of the box alive. Here at C.A.Gs we hold that line hard, and we count the clutches, because that single word is the whole reason a pair costs more than two singles. You are not paying for feathers. You are paying for the one question a single bird can never answer for you.</p>

  <div class="k3">
    <p class="k3-k">The Standard, in Five Lines</p>
    <ul class="k3-list">
      <li>Proven = a fertile clutch already hatched, and someone can tell you when.</li>
      <li>Bonded = the two birds tolerate and prefer each other. That is a start, not a record.</li>
      <li>Unproven = no clutch yet, for any reason, including age.</li>
      <li>Our three pairs are closed-banded, and we send the clutch history on request.</li>
      <li>Method is what we promise. A clutch on your schedule is not something anyone can promise.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <figure class="sec-img-wrap">
    <img class="sec-img inf-img" src="/images/breeding-pair/inf-1-what-proven-means.webp"
         srcset="/images/breeding-pair/inf-1-what-proven-means-760.webp 760w, /images/breeding-pair/inf-1-what-proven-means.webp 1408w"
         sizes="(max-width:980px) 92vw, 760px"
         alt="Chart showing what proven means on a parrot pair, compared against unproven and bonded only" width="1408" height="768" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
    <figcaption class="img-note">Only one of these three has hatched a fertile clutch. Ask which one you are being sold.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3 class="cag-h3">The Definition Working Breeders Use</h3>
  <p>Among people who actually run boxes, the word has a narrow meaning and nobody argues about it. A pair is proven when it has produced chicks that hatched. Eggs alone do not do it — a hen can lay a full clutch of clears every season for years, and clear eggs prove she is a hen and nothing more. We have watched newer buyers pay a proven-pair price for exactly that bird.</p>

  <h4 class="cag-h4">Proven, Unproven, and Simply Bonded</h4>
  <p>Three different states get sold under one word, and only one of them carries a record. Unproven is honest and usually cheap: two birds of confirmed sex, no clutch behind them. Bonded is a behavioral description of how they get along. Proven is a production fact with dates attached. A seller who slides between the three in one paragraph is telling you something about their bookkeeping.</p>

  <h5 class="cag-h5">Why a Bonded Pair Is Not Automatically a Breeding Pair</h5>
  <p>Bonding and breeding are two separate hurdles, and the second one is taller. A bonded pair preens, feeds each other, sleeps flush against the same perch and screams when separated. None of that requires either bird to ever enter a nest box. We sell one companion pair here that is deeply bonded and has never laid a thing, and we say so on its page rather than letting the word do quiet work for us.</p>

  <h6 class="cag-h6">From the Nest Box: A Pair Can Bond for Years and Never Lay</h6>
  <p>We have had two Congos share a flight for three seasons, model behavior all the way through, and produce nothing. Nothing was wrong with either bird. The box placement was wrong, and when we moved it the following winter, that changed. The lesson we took is that a bond is a precondition, not a prediction, and anyone selling you the bond as if it were the outcome has skipped the hard part.</p>

  <h3 class="cag-h3">What a Seller Should Be Able to Show You</h3>
  <p>Ask for the paperwork before you ask for the price. A breeder who has actually produced from a pair can answer four questions without stalling: which bird is the cock, what the band numbers read, when each clutch hatched, and who fed the chicks. If any of those four produce a pause, you are talking to a reseller who bought the pair last month and is repeating what they were told.</p>

  <h4 class="cag-h4">Band Numbers, Clutch Dates, and Who Reared the Chicks</h4>
  <p>Our pairs are closed-banded, and the band is the anchor for everything else. A closed band goes on a chick in the first days of life and cannot be slipped on an adult later, so it ties the bird to a hatch year and a breeder. <a href="/how-to-avoid-african-grey-parrot-scams/">Our scam-avoidance guide</a> walks through the rest of the document trail. We keep clutch dates in writing and we send them on request, and we will tell you plainly which chicks we pulled and hand-fed ourselves.</p>
</section>

<div class="seam-wrap" aria-hidden="true"><img class="seam" src="/cag-fs-seam-emblem.webp" alt="" width="182" height="60" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></div>

<section id="ladder">
  <h2 class="cag-h2">Why Does the Pair With the Fewest Clutches Cost the Most?</h2>
  <p>It looks backwards on the price sheet, so here is the reasoning. Talker and Jane, twelve and ten years old, have hatched four clutches and sell for $3,000. Sally and Odin, four and five, have hatched one, and they sell for $5,500. The evidence you are buying is the clutch that proves the pair works; the asset you are buying is every season after that one. Older birds carry a longer record and a shorter runway, and the price follows the runway. We set the ladder this way so that nobody pays a premium for history they cannot use.</p>

  <div class="k5"><p class="k5-t">Four hatched clutches prove more than one does — but one clutch at age four proves the same thing with a decade of seasons still in front of it.</p></div>

  <figure class="sec-img-wrap">
    <img class="sec-img inf-img" src="/images/breeding-pair/inf-2-price-ladder.webp"
         srcset="/images/breeding-pair/inf-2-price-ladder-760.webp 760w, /images/breeding-pair/inf-2-price-ladder.webp 1408w"
         sizes="(max-width:980px) 92vw, 760px"
         alt="Price ladder showing our three Congo grey pairs, where fewer hatched clutches costs more" width="1408" height="768" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
    <figcaption class="img-note">Prices rise as clutch counts fall, because you are buying breeding years ahead.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3 class="cag-h3">You Are Buying Breeding Years, Not Past Output</h3>
  <p>The clutches already hatched belong to us. They are the proof, and proof is worth something, but you will never sell a chick from a clutch that hatched in 2021. What you take home is the seasons in front of the pair, and that number is set almost entirely by how old the two birds are on the day they land at your place. Read the ladder as a rental on remaining years, priced by how many are left. Worth keeping in view while you do: a Congo African Grey lives 40 to 60 years, so a pair bought at four and five will still be here long after the aviary you are building around them has changed shape twice.</p>

  <h4 class="cag-h4">How the Ladder Works Across Our Three Pairs</h4>
  <p>Three pairs, three points on the same curve, and the arithmetic is visible in both directions. Talker and Jane hold the longest record of the three and sit at the bottom of the price ladder at $3,000, because at twelve and ten they have the fewest seasons ahead. Mari and Lake, six and seven with two clutches hatched, sit in the middle at $4,500. Sally and Odin are the youngest and least-recorded pair we have, and they cost the most at $5,500 for exactly that reason. <a href="/african-grey-parrot-price/">Our full pricing guide</a> shows how these sit against our single-bird prices.</p>

  <h5 class="cag-h5">When the Cheapest Pair Is the Right Buy</h5>
  <p>Talker and Jane are the correct purchase more often than the price suggests, and we will argue for them out loud. A pair with four hatched clutches has been through the whole cycle repeatedly, and there is very little left to discover about whether they will use a box. If your goal this year is to learn the work — candling, pulling chicks, the timing of it — you want the pair that has already done it four times, not the one still writing its second chapter.</p>

  <h6 class="cag-h6">Breeder's Note: Most First-Time Aviaries Should Start at the Bottom of This Ladder</h6>
  <p>Twelve years in, our honest advice to a first aviary is to buy the record and not the runway. A first-time setup usually loses its first season to things that have nothing to do with the birds: a box hung too low, a room with too much traffic, a light schedule that swings. Those mistakes are cheaper to make with an experienced pair that shrugs them off than with a young pair you would rather not unsettle. Buy the runway on your second pair, once your room has proven itself too.</p>

  <p class="ctaline"><a href="#reserve" class="btn-clay">Reserve a proven pair</a></p>
</section>

