<section id="age">
  <h2 class="cag-h2">How Old Must African Greys Be Before They Can Breed?</h2>
  <p>Sexual maturity in African Greys arrives around four years, and the earliest fertile eggs on record show up near three. Steady production usually comes later still, with most pairs settling into a rhythm around five or six. That gap between "capable" and "producing" is the number that matters to a buyer, because it is the gap a young pair still has to cross on your feed bill. Knowing it also gives you a fast test for any listing you are shown.</p>

  <figure class="sec-img-wrap">
    <img class="sec-img inf-img" src="/images/breeding-pair/inf-3-breeding-age-timeline.webp"
         srcset="/images/breeding-pair/inf-3-breeding-age-timeline-760.webp 760w, /images/breeding-pair/inf-3-breeding-age-timeline.webp 1408w"
         sizes="(max-width:980px) 92vw, 760px"
         alt="Timeline of how old African greys must be before they can breed, from three to six years" width="1408" height="768" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
    <figcaption class="img-note">Fertile eggs are on record at three years. A fifteen-month "proven" pair cannot exist.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3 class="cag-h3">Maturity, and the Gap Before the First Fertile Clutch</h3>
  <p>A grey reaching maturity is not a grey ready to raise chicks. The bird's body arrives at the threshold first and the behavior catches up afterward, which is why young pairs so often produce a clear clutch, then a broken one, then finally a good one. Psittacus erithacus is a slow species in every direction — slow to fledge, slow to mature, long-lived at the far end. <a href="https://www.parrots.org/encyclopedia/grey-parrot" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The World Parrot Trust's Grey Parrot species page</a> lays out the general life history behind that pattern.</p>

  <h4 class="cag-h4">What the Published Record Says</h4>
  <p>Tony Silva, whose aviculture records are among the most cited in the field, documents fertile eggs from greys at roughly three years of age, with sexual maturity around four and consistent production commonly arriving at five to six. Those are the published outer edges, not a schedule your pair owes you. We treat three as the floor of what is physically possible and four as the age at which a first clutch stops being remarkable.</p>

  <h5 class="cag-h5">Why a Fifteen-Month "Proven Pair" Cannot Exist</h5>
  <p>Do the subtraction and the listing collapses. A pair advertised as proven at fifteen months would have had to hatch a clutch before either bird was old enough to lay a fertile egg, and no record supports that. This is the most useful arithmetic on the page, because it takes five seconds and it does not require you to see the birds. We have watched this exact claim run on marketplaces for years, unchallenged, mostly because buyers assume the seller knows something they do not.</p>

  <h6 class="cag-h6">From the Nest Box: Silva Records Fertile Eggs at Three Years</h6>
  <p>Three years is the earliest we would credit, and we have never seen it here. Our own youngest producing pair, Sally and Odin, hatched their first clutch with Sally at four — which is early by our aviary's standards and entirely ordinary against the published record. When a bird beats the average we say so and we do not build the price around it.</p>
</section>

