<section id="tame">
  <h2 class="cag-h2">Why Are Our Proven Pairs Still Tame When Most Breeder Stock Is Not?</h2>
  <p>Most breeder stock is not tame, and the sellers who are honest about it say so in the listing. Royal Bird Company describes one of its own African Greys as a proven male that is not tame, that growls when approached, and that has not been handled in fifteen years. That is a normal outcome, not a scandal — and it is also what you inherit, every day, for the life of the bird. Ours came out differently because they started differently: every one of our six breeders was hand-reared here before it was ever paired.</p>

  <figure class="sec-img-wrap">
    <img class="sec-img og-photo og-tall" src="/images/breeding-pair/tame-hand-raised-african-grey-breeder-stock.webp"
         srcset="/images/breeding-pair/tame-hand-raised-african-grey-breeder-stock-760.webp 760w, /images/breeding-pair/tame-hand-raised-african-grey-breeder-stock.webp 1408w"
         sizes="(max-width:980px) 92vw, 760px"
         alt="Hand-raised Congo greys that went on to breed, still tame and steady around people" width="1408" height="768" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
    <figcaption class="img-note">Ours were hand-reared before they were paired, which is why they stayed steady.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3 class="cag-h3">Hand-Reared Birds That Went On to Breed</h3>
  <p>These six were raised under The Benjamin Home-Raising Protocol, which is our own name for how we do it: chicks pulled and hand-fed, weaned onto solids without being rushed, and held back until the twelve-to-sixteen-week gate before they go anywhere. They were also handled under The Midland Socialization Method — family handling in the house, daily out-of-cage time, ordinary noise. Those are our labels for our own practice, not a certification anybody issued us. What they buy you is a breeder bird that learned people were boring long before it learned it was a parent.</p>

  <h4 class="cag-h4">What Parent-Fed Breeder Stock Is Usually Like</h4>
  <p>Parent-fed birds pulled straight into a breeding flight never form that association, and they are the majority of the breeder stock on the market. A typical bird of that background lunges at the cage front, growls when a hand comes near, and treats every service visit as a threat. You can absolutely run a productive aviary that way, and thousands of people do. But you will be catching birds with a towel for the next thirty years, and vet visits become a two-person operation with a plan.</p>

  <h5 class="cag-h5">Handling a Pair Without Disturbing the Nest</h5>
  <p>Tame does not mean you should be handling a pair mid-season, and here the two goals actually fight each other. Once a hen is sitting, we go in for food and water and nothing else. The value of tameness at that point is not that you get to hold them — it is that a nest check does not detonate the room, and that a bird you need to catch in December will step up instead of running the flight. Save the handling for the months between clutches.</p>

  <h6 class="cag-h6">Breeder's Note: Tame Is Our Account of Our Own Birds, Not a Claim About the Species</h6>
  <p>We want the boundary on this one stated plainly. Everything above describes six specific Congo greys that we hatched, fed and paired, and we are telling you how they behave with us. It is not a claim about African Greys generally, and it is not a promise about how any of them will act in a strange room in the first month. Ask us what a specific pair is like and you will get a specific answer about that pair.</p>

  <p class="ctaline"><a href="#reserve" class="btn-clay">Ask for the clutch record</a></p>
</section>

