<section id="moving">
  <h2 class="cag-h2">How Do You Move a Proven Pair Without Stopping Production?</h2>
  <p>Relocation stops production constantly, and the people who admit it are the ones worth listening to. Royal Bird Company writes that when they moved some of their own proven breeders, the results were not the same. We believe them, because we have done it too. The difference between a pair that resumes and a pair that sulks for a year is almost never the birds — it is how much was changed at once, and how fast someone reached for the nest box.</p>

  <figure class="sec-img-wrap">
    <img class="sec-img inf-img" src="/images/breeding-pair/inf-4-moving-a-pair.webp"
         srcset="/images/breeding-pair/inf-4-moving-a-pair-760.webp 760w, /images/breeding-pair/inf-4-moving-a-pair.webp 1408w"
         sizes="(max-width:980px) 92vw, 760px"
         alt="Five step process flow for relocating a producing parrot pair without stopping production" width="1408" height="768" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
    <figcaption class="img-note">Five steps, in order. The nest box is offered last, never first.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3 class="cag-h3">What Goes Wrong When a Pair Is Moved Badly</h3>
  <p>The classic failure is enthusiasm. A pair arrives on Tuesday, goes into an unfamiliar cage in a busy room, gets a new diet the same week, and finds a nest box waiting on the wall — and then the new owner checks it every day to see whether anything happened. What that pair learns in its first fortnight is that the box is a place where a face appears. Add a diet swap on top and you have changed food, light, sound, cage and neighbors simultaneously, with no way to tell which one is the problem.</p>

  <h4 class="cag-h4">The Settling Sequence We Use</h4>
  <p>We have moved producing pairs more times than we can count, and the ones that kept laying all had the same few weeks of being left alone. First, the pair goes into its permanent cage in a low-traffic room and stays there — no rearranging later. Second, we hold the diet they arrived on and change nothing for the first weeks. Third, we keep the light and dark hours steady rather than easing them into a new schedule. Fourth, we service food and water at the same hour daily so the birds can predict us. The nest box goes up last, once the pair is eating normally and ignoring us, and then we leave it alone.</p>

  <h5 class="cag-h5">Why We Never Promise a Clutch, and What We Do Promise</h5>
  <p>We do not guarantee production, and we will not be talked into it. Any breeder who guarantees a clutch in your first season is either guessing or planning to argue with you later. What we promise is method and record: the clutch history in writing, the birds shipped in two crates rather than one under IATA Live Animals Regulations, our 72-hour health guarantee, and Mark on the phone for the settling period. Outcome belongs to the birds.</p>

  <p class="ctaline"><a href="#reserve" class="btn-clay">Talk to Mark about breeder stock</a></p>
</section>

