<section id="pairing">
  <h2 class="cag-h2">Why Does Random Pairing Fail When Experienced Pairing Works?</h2>
  <p>Before: you buy two greys you like the look of, put them in a flight, and wait. Lafeber's veterinary guidance on pairing is blunt about what happens next — there is no way to predict if they will get along, so you introduce them and see what happens. After: you buy two birds that already chose each other and have hatched chicks together, and the entire question is closed on the day of purchase. The bridge between those two positions is the only real argument for buying a pair instead of two singles.</p>

  <figure class="sec-img-wrap">
    <img class="sec-img inf-img" src="/images/breeding-pair/inf-5-pairing-method.webp"
         srcset="/images/breeding-pair/inf-5-pairing-method-760.webp 760w, /images/breeding-pair/inf-5-pairing-method.webp 1408w"
         sizes="(max-width:980px) 92vw, 760px"
         alt="Comparison of random bird pairing against matching a cock to a hen by method" width="1408" height="768" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
    <figcaption class="img-note">The difference is not luck. It is whether anyone confirmed the sexes first.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3 class="cag-h3">What Hobbyists Try, and Why It Usually Does Not Take</h3>
  <p>Self-pairing fails in two ordinary ways, and both are expensive. The first is sex: greys are not visually dimorphic, so a hopeful owner buys two birds by head shape and eye ring and ends up with two hens, or two cocks, and a year lost before anybody checks. The second is temperament, and it does not care what the paperwork says. A Minnesota breeder, Shades of Greys, listed a fifteen-year-old hen at $2,500 with the plain explanation that their male was just not into having a companion — a bird in its prime, on the market, because the pairing did not take. That is what a failed self-pairing looks like written down.</p>

  <h4 class="cag-h4">How We Match a Cock to a Hen</h4>
  <p>Our matching is not a gift, it is bookkeeping plus patience. We know which of our birds is the hen because we watched her lay, and we know which is the cock the same way — the clutch itself settles the question that hobbyists are still guessing at. From there we house candidates within sight of each other before they ever share a cage, and we watch for the things that decide it: who feeds whom, who moves off a perch, whether either bird is spending the day pinned in a corner. Pairs that do not take get separated and tried again another season. The three pairs on this page are the ones that took, and that is the difference you are actually paying for.</p>

  <h5 class="cag-h5">What Happens to the Pairs That Do Not Take</h5>
  <p>They go back into the flight and wait, which is the part of this work nobody advertises. A cock and hen who ignored each other for a full season are not defective birds and they are not a loss — they are two singles who have not met the right partner yet, and several of the birds we now sell as companions came through exactly that route. What we will not do is leave an indifferent pair together and sell the arrangement as bonded because the photograph looks right.</p>

  <h6 class="cag-h6"><span class="h6k">From the Nest Box:</span> A Season Spent Waiting Costs Less Than a Pair Sold Wrong</h6>
  <p>We would rather carry a bird for another year than hand a breeder two greys who will sit at opposite ends of a flight until they are returned. That is not generosity, it is arithmetic — a pair sold wrong comes back, and it comes back angrier than it left.</p>
</section>
<section id="market">
  <div class="seam-wrap" aria-hidden="true"><img class="seam" src="/cag-fs-seam-emblem.webp" alt="" width="182" height="60" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></div>
  <h2 class="cag-h2">What Does a Proven African Grey Pair Cost Elsewhere in the United States?</h2>
  <p>A proven pair of Congo African Greys is one of the few purchases in this hobby where the sticker price tells you almost nothing on its own. Two sellers can both write "proven pair" and mean completely different animals — one has a hen who has raised four clutches under a band you can read, the other has two adult greys who happened to share a flight and once produced a cracked egg. We have bred Congos in Midland since 2014, and the question we field most from people shopping around is why our three pairs sit under almost everything listed nationally. Here are the actual numbers.</p>

  <h3 class="cag-h3">What Documented Pairs Are Currently Listed At</h3>
  <p>We pulled these from live, dated listings on birdsnow, quoted exactly as they appeared rather than as a range we invented afterward. A pair in Tulsa, Oklahoma was listed at $9,495. Stone Mountain, Georgia was $8,000. Gwynnoak, Maryland was $6,500. Another Texas seller had a proven pair at $5,500. Ours run $3,000 to $5,500 out of Midland — Talker and Jane at $3,000 with four clutches behind them, Lake and Mari at $4,500 with two, Odin and Sally at $5,500 with one. That is the whole range, top to bottom.</p>

  <figure class="sec-img-wrap">
    <img class="sec-img inf-img" src="/images/breeding-pair/inf-6-market-prices.webp"
         srcset="/images/breeding-pair/inf-6-market-prices-760.webp 760w, /images/breeding-pair/inf-6-market-prices.webp 1408w"
         sizes="(max-width:980px) 92vw, 760px"
