    <figcaption class="img-note">Live listings from Tulsa, Stone Mountain, Gwynnoak and Texas, set beside ours.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h4 class="cag-h4">Why Ours Sit Below Those Numbers</h4>
  <p>The reason is boring, and we would rather give you the boring true answer than a clever one: we bred these birds ourselves. Talker, Jane, Lake, Mari, Odin and Sally all came up in our aviary, so there is no acquisition cost sitting underneath our price and no middle party who needs to clear a margin on top of what they paid somebody else. We only have to price above what it costs us to keep a pair fed, housed and healthy. That is the entire gap. Our prices do not move, there is no was-now framing anywhere on this site, and nobody has ever gotten a different number out of us by asking twice.</p>

  <h5 class="cag-h5">Why a Low Price With No Paperwork Is Still a Warning Sign</h5>
  <p>Cheap is not the same as honest, and we will not pretend otherwise just because we are the cheaper listing on this page. A $3,000 pair with readable band numbers and a written clutch record is a different purchase from a $3,000 pair advertised in three sentences by somebody who will not photograph the birds on their own perch. If a seller cannot produce bands, hatch records and CITES captive-bred documentation, the low price is not a bargain — it is the cost of the missing paperwork, moved onto you.</p>

