<section id="housing">
  <div class="seam-wrap" aria-hidden="true"><img class="seam" src="/cag-fs-seam-emblem.webp" alt="" width="182" height="60" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></div>
  <h2 class="cag-h2">What Housing and Nest Box Does a Breeding Pair Need?</h2>
  <p>Before a pair leaves here we want to know where they are going, and the setup question is the one that decides it. A breeding pair is not a companion bird in a bigger cage. They need room to fly, a box they can defend, and air that is not fighting them. The World Parrot Trust publishes housing figures for <em>Psittacus erithacus</em> that match what twelve years in our own flights has taught us, and we hold every buyer to them because a pair in the wrong enclosure will simply stop — no eggs, more feather damage, and a hen who spends her day on the cage floor.</p>

  <h3 class="cag-h3">Flight Space Before Nest Box</h3>
  <p>People get this backwards constantly. They buy the nest box first, mount it on a parrot cage from a big-box store, and then wonder why nothing happens for two years. Flight length comes first and it is not negotiable in our sale: three metres, which is 9.8 feet, is the minimum run a pair needs to actually fly rather than hop. Temperature and humidity matter nearly as much, and in West Texas the humidity number is the one that catches people out — our dry summers will pull a flight well under range without a humidifier running. These four figures follow <a href="https://www.parrottrust.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">World Parrot Trust</a> guidance for <em>Psittacus erithacus</em>, not our preference.</p>

  <table class="tH">
    <caption>The Four Housing Specifications We Require</caption>
    <thead>
      <tr><th scope="col">Specification</th><th scope="col">Requirement</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td data-label="Specification">Flight length</td><td data-label="Requirement">3 metres minimum (9.8 feet)</td></tr>
      <tr><td data-label="Specification">Nest box</td><td data-label="Requirement">12 × 12 × 24 inches, vertical, mounted outside the cage</td></tr>
      <tr><td data-label="Specification">Temperature</td><td data-label="Requirement">68–80&deg;F</td></tr>
      <tr><td data-label="Specification">Humidity</td><td data-label="Requirement">50–65%</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p><a href="https://www.parrots.org/encyclopedia/grey-parrot" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The World Parrot Trust's Grey Parrot species entry</a> is where those figures come from, and we would rather you read it directly than take our word for it. Nothing on that page is proprietary to us. It is simply the clearest published summary of what the species needs, and a buyer who has read it asks us better questions.</p>

  <figure class="sec-img-wrap">
    <img class="sec-img inf-img" src="/images/breeding-pair/inf-7-housing-nest-box.webp"
         srcset="/images/breeding-pair/inf-7-housing-nest-box-760.webp 760w, /images/breeding-pair/inf-7-housing-nest-box.webp 1408w"
         sizes="(max-width:980px) 92vw, 760px"
         alt="Housing and nest box specifications a breeding parrot pair needs, with flight length and humidity" width="1408" height="768" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
    <figcaption class="img-note">Flight length comes before the nest box. A box in a small cage is not a setup.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h4 class="cag-h4">Nest Box Dimensions and Placement</h4>
  <p>Our boxes are 12 by 12 by 24 inches, oriented vertically, and mounted on the outside of the flight rather than inside it. Vertical matters because a grey hen wants depth and darkness at the bottom, not a shallow tray. Outside mounting matters for two reasons: it gives the pair back the floor space the box would have stolen, and it lets you inspect through an access hatch without putting your arm into the space a defensive cock bird is guarding. Add a few inches of pine shavings, leave the pair alone, and resist the urge to check it hourly. Greys are private about this, and a hen who feels watched is a hen who abandons.</p>

  <p class="ctaline"><a href="#reserve" class="btn-clay">Talk to Mark about breeder stock</a></p>
</section>

<section id="paperwork">
  <div class="seam-wrap" aria-hidden="true"><img class="seam" src="/cag-fs-seam-emblem.webp" alt="" width="182" height="60" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></div>
  <h2 class="cag-h2">What Paperwork Comes With Each Pair?</h2>
  <p>Congo African Greys are CITES Appendix I, uplisted at CoP17 and effective January 2017, and the IUCN lists the species as Endangered. That places real documentation obligations on anyone breeding or selling them, and it is the part of this transaction where we are least casual. Every bird we sell is captive-bred here in our own facility. Below is what travels with a pair — and, just as importantly, what does not.</p>

  <h3 class="cag-h3">Closed Bands and the Clutch Record</h3>
  <p>Both birds in every pair wear a closed band with a readable number, and a closed band makes a specific claim: it goes on a chick's leg in the first days of life and cannot be slipped onto an adult later. That is the whole point of it — physical evidence that a bird hatched in captivity rather than being imported and re-labeled. Alongside the bands we keep the clutch record for each pair, dates and counts and what hatched, and we send it before you pay anything.</p>

  <figure class="sec-img-wrap">
    <img class="sec-img og-photo og-tall" src="/images/breeding-pair/proven-african-grey-hen-on-nest-with-eggs.webp"
         srcset="/images/breeding-pair/proven-african-grey-hen-on-nest-with-eggs-760.webp 760w, /images/breeding-pair/proven-african-grey-hen-on-nest-with-eggs.webp 1408w"
         sizes="(max-width:980px) 92vw, 760px"
         alt="A proven Congo grey hen sitting her clutch inside the nest box in our aviary" width="1408" height="768" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
    <figcaption class="img-note">A sitting hen in our nest boxes, photographed without disturbing the nest.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h4 class="cag-h4">What We Hold, and What We Do Not</h4>
  <p>Here is the full list, and we would rather it be short and true than long and padded. We hold closed bands with numbers on both birds, the clutch record available on request, CITES Appendix I captive-bred documentation, and hatch records for all six of these adults. We run a USDA Animal Welfare Act licensed facility. <a href="https://cites.org/eng/app/appendices.php" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The CITES appendices themselves</a> are published openly if you want to confirm the Appendix I listing rather than trust a breeder's summary of it.</p>

  <h5 class="cag-h5">Why These Pairs Carry No DNA Certificate</h5>
  <p>Plainly: these three pairs do not come with a DNA certificate, and we are not going to imply otherwise by staying quiet about it. Nobody sent a feather or a blood card to a lab for Talker, Jane, Lake, Mari, Odin or Sally. We know their sexes because we have watched them breed, and every one of the six has been part of a clutch we recorded. A lab result tells you which bird is the hen. It does not tell you whether she will sit, whether the cock will feed her, or whether the two tolerate each other well enough to raise anything.</p>

  <h6 class="cag-h6">Clutch Record: Four Hatched Clutches Is Itself a Demonstration of a Functional Pair</h6>
  <p>Talker and Jane have raised four clutches, and that record does more work than a certificate could, because it is a functional test rather than a genetic one — four separate times, this cock and this hen went to nest and brought chicks out of the box. Lake and Mari have done it twice. Odin and Sally once. Seven clutches across the three pairs. If you want the document for your own records, run a test after the birds arrive; any avian lab will take the sample. We are simply not going to charge you for paperwork we did not produce.</p>

  <p class="ctaline"><a href="#reserve" class="btn-clay">Ask for the clutch record</a></p>
</section>

<section id="travel">
  <div class="seam-wrap" aria-hidden="true"><img class="seam" src="/cag-fs-seam-emblem.webp" alt="" width="182" height="60" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></div>
  <h2 class="cag-h2">How Does an African Grey Breeding Pair Travel to You?</h2>
  <p>Moving two bonded adult greys across the country is a different job from shipping a weaned baby, and we plan it that way. Adults are heavier, stronger, and far more inclined to take out their stress on whatever is nearest — which, in a shared crate, is each other. We fly pairs as cargo on Delta, United and American under the IATA Live Animals Regulations, and we book the routing ourselves rather than handing you a tracking number and wishing you luck.</p>

  <h3 class="cag-h3">Two Crates, Never One</h3>
  <p>A pair travels in two crates. Not one large crate, not one crate with a divider — two separate crates, every time, with no exceptions we have ever made. Bonded birds who are perfectly civil to each other in a three-metre flight are a genuine risk to one another inside a dark box on a loud tarmac, and a bitten foot or a broken blood feather at 30,000 feet has no remedy until landing. The two crates fly on the same aircraft and arrive together, so the pair is separated for hours, not days. <a href="https://www.iata.org/en/programs/cargo/live-animals/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The IATA Live Animals Regulations</a> govern the container standards, and every crate we send meets them.</p>

  <figure class="sec-img-wrap">
    <img class="sec-img og-photo og-scene" src="/images/breeding-pair/african-grey-breeding-pair-two-crate-shipping.webp"
         srcset="/images/breeding-pair/african-grey-breeding-pair-two-crate-shipping-760.webp 760w, /images/breeding-pair/african-grey-breeding-pair-two-crate-shipping.webp 1408w"
         sizes="(max-width:980px) 92vw, 760px"
         alt="Airline shipping crates used to fly a bonded parrot pair to buyers across the United States" width="1408" height="768" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
    <figcaption class="img-note">A pair always travels in two crates. One crate for two adults is how birds get hurt.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h4 class="cag-h4">Airport Cargo, Home Delivery, or Midland Pickup</h4>
  <p>Three ways to get your pair, and the right one depends mostly on how far you are from us. Airport cargo is $185 — we fly the birds into your nearest served airport and you collect them from the cargo counter, usually within a couple of hours of landing. Home delivery is $350 and ends with the crates at your door. Pickup in Midland, Texas is free and is what we recommend if you are within two or three hours of us, because it removes the flight entirely and lets you see the flight the pair came out of. Both shipping figures are flat and cover the whole pair, not each bird.</p>

  <p>The birds ship with their documentation, and the paperwork does not follow separately in the mail a week later. Band numbers, hatch records and CITES captive-bred documentation travel with the crates.</p>

  <h5 class="cag-h5">What We Need From You Before a Pair Flies</h5>
  <p>Weather is the part neither of us controls, and it decides more travel dates than anything else. Airlines hold live-animal embargoes when it is too hot or too cold at either end, so we need your nearest cargo-served airport and a rough window rather than a fixed date, and we book to the first morning that clears at both ends. Have the flight ready before the pair lands, not after — a cage still in a box is the most common reason a settled pair arrives to an unsettled start.</p>

  <h6 class="cag-h6"><span class="h6k">Breeder's Note:</span> We Would Rather Move a Date Than Fly Into Bad Weather</h6>
  <p>If the forecast turns, we will call and push the flight, and we will keep the pair here at our cost until it clears. Nobody has ever been unhappy about that call a week later.</p>
</section>

<section id="singles">
  <div class="seam-wrap" aria-hidden="true"><img class="seam" src="/cag-fs-seam-emblem.webp" alt="" width="182" height="60" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></div>
  <h2 class="cag-h2">Which Single Greys and Fertile Eggs Can You Add?</h2>
  <p>Not everybody who lands on this page needs two adults in a flight. Some of you are building an aviary and want to add unrelated bloodline to what you already keep. Some of you incubate your own and would rather start at the egg. And a fair number of you came here looking for a talking companion and found breeder stock by accident, which is a different bird entirely. We keep singles and eggs available alongside the pairs for exactly those reasons.</p>

  <h3 class="cag-h3">Available Singles From Our Own Lines</h3>
  <p>Every single we offer came out of our own flights, hand-fed here and raised under the same two methods we apply to every chick. <strong>The Benjamin Home-Raising Protocol</strong> is our name for how we hand-feed, wean and hold a bird to the 12–16-week gate — no chick leaves before it is eating on its own and holding weight. <strong>The Midland Socialization Method</strong> is the second half: family handling, daily out-of-cage routine, and household noise, so a bird is not startled by a vacuum on day one. Neither label is a third-party certification and we have never claimed one; they are what we call our own process. <a href="/congo-african-grey-for-sale/">Our Congo singles</a> — Bery, Amie and Roys — sit in the $1,500 to $3,500 range that runs across the site. <a href="/timneh-african-grey-for-sale/">Our two Timnehs</a>, Elad at $1,600 and Evie at $1,500, are the smaller, rustier-tailed cousin and often the better first grey.</p>

  <h3 class="cag-h3">Candled Fertile Eggs for Incubating Breeders</h3>
  <p>For breeders who run their own incubator, we sell candled fertile eggs from these same lines. Candled means we have held the egg to light and confirmed development before it is offered — we do not sell eggs on hope. This is the option for someone who already has the equipment, the brooder and the hand-feeding experience, and who would rather bring chicks up under their own protocol than buy a producing pair outright. It is also, honestly, the option with the most ways to go wrong, so we talk it through with every buyer rather than just boxing eggs. Ask us for current availability; egg supply moves with what is in our boxes that month.</p>

  <figure class="sec-img-wrap">
    <img class="sec-img og-photo og-scene" src="/images/breeding-pair/candled-fertile-african-grey-eggs-incubator.webp"
         srcset="/images/breeding-pair/candled-fertile-african-grey-eggs-incubator-760.webp 760w, /images/breeding-pair/candled-fertile-african-grey-eggs-incubator.webp 1408w"
         sizes="(max-width:980px) 92vw, 760px"
         alt="Candled fertile grey parrot eggs in an incubator, available to breeders who hatch their own" width="1408" height="768" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
    <figcaption class="img-note">For breeders who would rather incubate than buy a pair outright.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <div class="xsell">
    <p class="xsell-k">Not Buying Breeder Stock?</p>
    <p>A proven pair is the wrong bird for a living room, and we say that to people every week. Talker and Jane are bonded to each other, not to you — that is exactly what makes them worth $3,000 as breeders and a poor fit as a companion. If you want a grey who talks to you and learns your household, a hand-raised single is the bird you came for. Bery, Amie and Roys are Congos out of our lines; Elad and Evie are Timnehs. Same aviary, same protocol, completely different job.</p>
  </div>

  <p class="ctaline"><a href="#reserve" class="btn-clay">Reserve a proven pair</a></p>
</section>
        <div class="seam-wrap" aria-hidden="true"><img class="seam" src="/cag-fs-seam-emblem.webp" alt="" width="182" height="60" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></div>

